course: dictionary
lesson: 9326
title: Spicy: hot food and juicy news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Spicy food has a strong, hot or rich taste because it contains spices such as chilli or pepper. Food that is too spicy can burn your mouth, as when Ming's family says «tài là le!» (too spicy!).
explain: Spicy can also describe a story or news that is a bit shocking, exciting or naughty. «She heard some spicy gossip about the school play.» This is an informal meaning, the way people chat about things they should not share too widely.
explain: Use the clues around the word. If the sentence is about eating, tasting or your mouth, spicy means hot taste. If it is about news, gossip or a story, it means exciting or a bit naughty. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Spicy is an adjective. Its comparative forms are spicier and spiciest, and the noun is spiciness.
fact: Spicy food has a strong, hot or rich taste because it contains spices such as chilli or pepper.
fact: Chilli peppers come from the Americas and reached Asia in the 1500s with Portuguese and other traders.
fact: Red, spicy kimchi became common in Korea in the 1700s.
fact: «tài là le» («TIGH LAH luh») means too spicy, and là means chilli-hot.
exercise: «The soup was so spicy that Sam reached for a glass of water.» What does spicy describe here? | *A strong, hot taste | A noisy, busy kitchen | A cold, sweet taste
exercise: Ming's friend says, «The news about the school trip is spicy!» What does spicy mean here? | *Exciting, and a bit shocking or naughty | Very cold and wet | Made with lots of chilli | Very slow to arrive
exercise: Which word is the noun for spicy? | spicily | spicest | *spiciness | spicing
test: Where do chilli peppers come from? | *The Americas | Scotland | Ivory Coast | Korea
test: «tài là le» means what? | *Too spicy | Too sweet to eat | Too cold to drink | Too loud to hear
exam: Grandma says the curry is spicy, and Tom says the gossip is spicy too. In which sentence does spicy mean shocking or naughty? | Both sentences | Grandma's, about the curry | Neither sentence | *Tom's, about the gossip
